<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  The rest of Info.plist is generated from the INFOPLIST_KEY_* build
	  settings in project.yml; Xcode merges that generated content onto this
	  base file, which holds what the generator cannot express.

	  This app's own URL scheme, under the URL type named PrimitiveAuth.
	  `primitive init` stamps an app-unique scheme in place of `primitiveapp`:
	  every app scaffolded from this template would otherwise register the same
	  one, and iOS picks arbitrarily between two apps claiming a scheme.

	  Sign-in email: CODE-ONLY BY DEFAULT, and that works with no configuration
	  at all. The client sends no link target, so the server never consults the
	  allow-list.

	  IF THIS APP HAS A WEB COUNTERPART, prefer the https link: add
	  "webUrl": "https://your-web-domain" to the environment in
	  primitive/config.json (a bare origin) and the sign-in email carries
	  "https://your-web-domain/oauth/callback". That one link opens THIS app
	  when it is installed (a universal link, once the domain serves
	  apple-app-site-association and the applinks entitlement names it) and is
	  an ordinary web sign-in in any browser. Allow-list that https URL the
	  same way. The full checklist is in the authentication guide.

	  A custom-scheme LINK instead takes two steps, and no source edit:

	    1. Merge "<scheme>://oauth/callback" into the existing
	       [auth].emailRedirectUris array in your app's app.toml and push the
	       app settings with `primitive config push` (its option flag spells a
	       double hyphen, which an XML comment cannot contain; the exact
	       command is in docs/README.md).
	    2. Add the PrimitiveOAuthRedirectURI key below this comment, with the
	       same "<scheme>://oauth/callback" as its string. The app passes it
	       to the client as its oauthRedirectUri, which points the emailed
	       link there and accepts the callback when it comes back.

	  What each missing piece looks like, so a half-wired app is diagnosable:
	    - key set, URI not allow-listed  → EVERY email request fails 400
	                                       `Invalid redirect URI`
	    - allow-listed, key not set      → the email stays code-only
	    - scheme not registered here     → the emailed link is a dead tap;
	                                       nothing launches, nothing logs

	  A custom-scheme link only opens on a device that has this app installed:
	  dead in the Simulator, dead cross-device, and most webmail clients will
	  not render a non-http(s) href as a link, which is exactly what the https
	  link above fixes. The code is the credential that always works.
	-->
	<key>CFBundleURLTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>CFBundleURLName</key>
			<string>PrimitiveAuth</string>
			<key>CFBundleURLSchemes</key>
			<array>
				<string>primitiveapp</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
